        "excerpt_selection.md": "# Excerpt Selection: TX_BBO_XX_G5_1.0_8_v1\n\nGenerated: 2026-05-04\n\n## Selection Metadata\n- **Curriculum:** Bluebonnet (TX_BBO_XX)\n- **Grade:** G5\n- **Lesson ID:** TX_BBO_XX_G5_1.0_8_v1\n- **Standard:** TEKS 5.10.D\n- **Learning Goal:** Students identify similes and metaphors in \"A Boy Goes to Washington.\"\n- **Reading Range (from planning doc):** \"A Boy Goes to Washington\" — All of Paragraph 2 and All of Paragraph 10\n\n## Excerpt Scope\n- **Selected Paragraphs:** Paragraph 2 and Paragraph 10 (source numbering; activity sequential numbers (1) and (2))\n- **Excerpt Structure:** Multiple labeled excerpts (two non-contiguous paragraphs from the same text)\n- **Total Length:** ~134 words (~2 paragraphs)\n- **Scope Source:** Module plan `exact_text_excerpt` column (author-specified exact paragraphs)\n- **Confidence:** High (author pre-selected exact paragraphs; confirmed by `pre_selected_excerpt_cues` in requirements.json)\n- **Paragraphs excluded from author-specified range:** None — excerpt covers the full author-specified range. Author specified only these two paragraphs; no other paragraphs were designated.\n\n## Numbering\n- **Curriculum behavior:** `sequential` — from excerpt_numbering_rules.md lookup (Bluebonnet / TX_BBO row confirmed)\n- **Numbering authority:** Sequential count (no embedded source numbering; no teacher edition with paragraph anchors available)\n- **Teacher edition anchors found:** No — lesson plan PDF not present in pipeline; no teacher edition paragraph references available\n- **Source numbering detected:** No — the anthology `.txt` file has no embedded paragraph numbers\n- **Convention applied:** sequential from (1); multi-excerpt activity, numbering continuous across excerpts\n- **Activity range:** (1)–(2)\n- **Source range:** Source paragraphs 2 and 10 → renumbered to (1) and (2)\n- **Boundary notes:** The source `.txt` file contains page-number artifacts (e.g., bare \"20\", \"21\", \"22\", \"23\", \"24\" on their own lines) and `[image]` markers. These are page markers / image placeholders, not paragraphs — they were skipped during boundary counting. Paragraph 4 (source) spans a page break with \"20\" and \"[image]\" inserted mid-paragraph; the paragraph continues after the break as a single block. Paragraph 9 (source) similarly spans a page break at \"23\" / \"[image]\". No boundary ambiguities in paragraphs 2 or 10 specifically — both are clearly delimited by blank lines with no mid-paragraph page breaks.\n- **Extraction method:** `pdftotext-fallback` — No source PDF exists for \"A Boy Goes to Washington\" in `source_material/texts/Bluebonnet/G5/M1/`. Only a `.txt` anthology extraction is present (`Anthology_G5_A Boy Goes to Washington.txt`). The Shinpei Mykawa Split-Chapters PDFs in M1 are a different text. `pdf_extract_styled.py` was not applicable. Fallback to `.txt` is correct here.\n- **Italics confidence:** `not-applicable` — No source PDF available for styled extraction; `.txt` file carries no italic information. Any italics in the print anthology would not be reflected in the extracted text.\n\n## Rationale\nThe author pre-selected these two paragraphs in the module plan's `exact_text_excerpt` column, specifying \"All of Paragraph 2\" and \"All of Paragraph 10.\" Paragraph 2 contains the metaphor targeted by Driving Question 3 and the lesson plan activity (\"That train rolled on all day long, to the song of the metal against the tracks, the blur of the towns and villages moving past my eyes\"), where the train's sound is compared to a song without using \"like\" or \"as.\" Paragraph 10 contains the simile targeted by the Target Task (\"When the train pulled into Washington, I had never seen so many lights. It was like the world was on fire\"), where the lights of Washington are compared to fire using \"like.\" Both selections are confirmed by the `pre_selected_excerpt_cues` in `requirements.json`. No other paragraphs were designated by the author.\n\n## Supplemental Guidance Applied\nPipeline-mode shortcut #2 used: read `_pipeline/curriculum_context.md` instead of `curriculum_defaults.csv`. Key guidance applied:\n- Numbering behavior: `sequential` (confirmed from curriculum_context.md Bluebonnet rules)\n- Extraction method: anthology `.txt` only (no PDF for this specific text); `italics_confidence: not-applicable` noted\n- Trailing biographical paragraphs rule (Step 4 rule 6): not applicable here — author specified only two paragraphs; trailing biographical content (paragraphs 12–13 in source) was not part of the designated excerpt scope\n\n---\n\n## Numbering Manifest\n\n| # | First words | Word count |\n|---|------------|------------|\n| (1) | \"My mind was closed to all things past…\" | 96 |\n| (2) | \"When the train pulled into Washington, I had never…\" | 38 |\n\n**Note:** Source paragraphs numbered by sequential prose-paragraph count from the start of the text body (excluding title, byline, and all page-number artifacts). Source paragraph 2 = activity paragraph (1); source paragraph 10 = activity paragraph (2).\n\n---\n\n## Extracted Text\n\n**Excerpt 1: \"A Boy Goes to Washington,\" Paragraph 2**\n\n(1) My mind was closed to all things past. I was not going to look back on my farm days, hog slopping, or the long hours of toil. I didn't give a thought to things back at home. That train rolled on all day long, to the song of the metal against the tracks, the blur of the towns and villages moving past my eyes. I wasn't going to get to Washington until around eight o'clock that night, and I told the porter that I would be glad to work if he needed anyone to help him.\n\n**Excerpt 2: \"A Boy Goes to Washington,\" Paragraph 10**\n\n(2) When the train pulled into Washington, I had never seen so many lights. It was like the world was on fire. As I gathered my stuff, the porter asked me again if I wanted the train job.\n",
        "excerpt_text.md": "# Excerpt Text: TX_BBO_XX_G5_1.0_8_v1\n\nGenerated: 2026-05-04\nNumbering convention: sequential\nFormat: (N)\nTotal paragraphs: 2\n\n---\n\n**Excerpt 1: \"A Boy Goes to Washington,\" Paragraph 2**\n\n(1) My mind was closed to all things past. I was not going to look back on my farm days, hog slopping, or the long hours of toil. I didn't give a thought to things back at home. That train rolled on all day long, to the song of the metal against the tracks, the blur of the towns and villages moving past my eyes. I wasn't going to get to Washington until around eight o'clock that night, and I told the porter that I would be glad to work if he needed anyone to help him.\n\n**Excerpt 2: \"A Boy Goes to Washington,\" Paragraph 10**\n\n(2) When the train pulled into Washington, I had never seen so many lights. It was like the world was on fire. As I gathered my stuff, the porter asked me again if I wanted the train job.\n",
